Z||000047||Haze_Performance||12-17-2001||10:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pyric:<BR><B>Well, it looks like it may be an 84, and I recall reading somewhere that they didn't make a red fiero in 84.<P>I'm probably wrong though.<P>I've also never seen a Fiero with a back bumper with rubber pads on it, but then again, I may have just not been paying attention.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They did make Red 84's (I have 1)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>& All 84/86 Coupes have front and rear black bumper pads .  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||14
Z||000048||Hartz||12-17-2001||11:02 PM||||Credit one pint of cyber beer to T-tops - with a partial, half pint credit to Pyric for the nice art work!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Yes, they made red '84's (I have one myself), they made them without luggage racks or wings, and they made them with little black bumper pads (not rubber, BTW.)<P>However, this particular car is missing the air intake, and no, the picture is not flipped (Proof I didn't flip it?  One, I'm not that good with photo editing, and two, the lettering on the pace car is right, as is the location of the gas door.)<P>This car belonged (belongs?) to Pontiac.  I remember the story being that this was the first driveable, pre-production prototype.  Not the first production car (which I think I read somewhere was white) but rather the first prototype - and they hadn't decided on putting in the air intake yet.  I guess that came later.<P>Nice catch guys!<P>Hartz||||reg||1
